Logic :

Find the length of the array and store it in len.
Handle the special case when the array has only one element.
Use cyclic sort logic to place each positive number at its correct index.
Traverse the array using index i starting from 1.
Store the current element in temp to find its correct position.
Check if the current element is already in the correct position (arr[i - 1] == i).
Ignore elements that are less than 1 because only positive numbers are considered.
Ignore elements greater than len because they cannot be the first missing positive number.
Ignore duplicate values to avoid infinite swapping.
If the element is valid and not in its correct position, swap it with the element at its correct index (arr[temp - 1]).
Continue the process until all possible positive numbers are placed at their correct indices.
Traverse the array again from index 1.
Check each position to see if the value matches its expected index.
If a mismatch is found, return that index because it is the smallest missing positive number.
If all positions contain the correct values, return len + 1 because the next number is missing.

Code :

class Solution {
    public int firstMissingPositive(int[] arr) {
        int len=arr.length;
        int i=1;
        if(arr.length==1){
            if(arr[i-1]==1){
            return arr[i-1]+1;
            }
            else{
                return 1;
            }
        }
        
        for(i=1;i<=len;){
            int temp=arr[i-1];
            if(arr[i-1]==i || arr[i-1]<1 || arr[i-1]>len || arr[i-1]==arr[temp-1]){
                i++;
            }
            else{
                
                arr[i-1]=arr[temp-1];
                arr[temp-1]=temp;
            }
        }
            for(i=1;i<=len;){
                if(arr[i-1]==i){
                    i++;
                }
                else{
                    break;
                }
               
            }
        

         return i;
        
    }
}
